  "summary_text": "Kyle, Monday was the first full day at both locations post-launch, and the picture is structurally constrained by capacity\u2014not performance. Port Washington ran two groomers and produced $528 per groomer, 4% above the trailing-30-day baseline; Hicksville ran one groomer at $240, which is 37% below baseline and the real operational concern. Both stores are severely understaffed relative to prior-month scheduling (Port down from 6.0 to 2.0 groomer-days; Hicksville down from 3.0 to 1.0). The good news: Port Washington's 82% add-on attach rate with just two groomers shows your team is executing the upsell discipline even in a lean state. You should be proud of that conversion rigor.\n\nThe structural headwind is real. Port Washington's MTD revenue of $1,859 against a DOW-adjusted expectation of $2,547 leaves a -27% gap\u2014but that gap is almost entirely driven by groomer availability, not demand or pricing. Hicksville is worse: -49% against adjusted expectation, with zero bather hours yesterday and only one groomer on the floor. Both locations are also signaling inventory stress (X-Small Treat Table backordered at both sites; Hicksville's lapse pool is drifting further out\u201412+ week gap growing 1.8 points, early warning of stealth churn).\n\nWhat matters now: Port Washington is scheduled to jump to 6 groomers today and stay loaded through the next week (30 groomer-days scheduled). Hicksville has only 11 groomer-days across the next seven days with zero bather support, which is insufficient to close the revenue gap and risks accelerating customer drift. You need to restock Hicksville's coverage plan immediately and reorder X-Small Treat Tables and braided bully chews at Port before further backorder damage.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today you have 8 total groomer-days (Port 6, Hicksville 2) with 0 bather hours; tomorrow 8 groomer-days with 7 bather hours; next 7 days 41 total groomer-days across both locations, weighted heavily to Port.**"
